hi i'm dr john anson i'm a neurosurgeon  working here at southern hills hospital  and this is showing you our  new augmented reality system for  spine surgery this system allows us to  actually  see the spine of a patient live during  surgery  it's like giving us x-ray vision it  starts with this headset  that has a heads-up display on it  so when i put the headset on i can look  through these  glass frames and actually  see the spine displayed on them even as  i look through  at the patient and at the instruments  that i'm using this array gets mounted  on the patient  and all of the instruments like this one  will have  another little array on them so i can  actually see  [Music]  exactly what i'm doing i'm showing the  computer  my instrument and what you're seeing on  the computer is what i'm seeing  in my up display so i can come in here  inside the patient's body so i don't  even need to make an incision  get to where i want to be  and get down right into the spine and  this is live in real time being able to  view  the actual anatomy as i'm doing surgery  we still do the same operations but it  allows us  to do them through much smaller  incisions and with  much more precise guidance  as to where we're going and what we're  doing it can be adapted to any variety  of instruments and  used up and down the spine we're the  first hospital in southern nevada to  have this technology  there's only a handful of hospitals  around the country in fact that have it  so i feel like we're really continuing  to be at the forefront of these new  technologies that allow us to do  better safer more precise and minimally  invasive surgery  i think it's going to help my patients a  lot i think it's going to make the  surgeries  better for them easier on them allow  better quicker easier recovery
 

